p256 — third-party attribution

This file carries a CONDITION, added 2026-09-06. `src/wycheproof_kat_vectors.zig`
and `src/wycheproof_der_kat_vectors.zig` reproduce 725 test vectors from the
Wycheproof project, which is published under the Apache License 2.0. Apache-2.0
§4 asks for three things MIT does not: that a copy of the License travel with
the material (§4(a)), that modified files say they were changed (§4(b)), and
that upstream copyright and attribution notices be retained (§4(c)). All three
are discharged here. Redistributing this module — alone, or as part of zig-libs
— must keep this file with it.

WHAT CHANGED AND WHY, 2026-09-06. Until this revision `modules/p256/NOTICE` did
not exist at all, and the two generated files above were headed
`// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT` with nothing anywhere in the module naming
their licence. `README.md`'s `Provenance:` statement answers a different
question — it is about the module's CODE ("clean-room from the NIST P-256
domain parameters", with OpenSSL's nistz256 read for technique shape only) —
and says nothing about the 358 315 bytes of reproduced third-party test DATA
that the module also ships. A consumer had no way to learn the obligation
existed.

The gate that exists for exactly this shape could not see it. `check-catalog`'s
data-file claim fires on a module carrying a committed file that is neither Zig
source nor documentation; it was written after `bitcoinscript` was found
shipping ~2000 rows of Bitcoin Core's `script_tests.json` unattributed.
`modules/p256/` has zero non-`.zig`/`.md` files: the vectors went through
`scripts/gen/gen-p256-wycheproof.py` and landed as `.zig`, so no witness was found
and the check never ran. Reproduced third-party data wearing a generated-source
extension is invisible to it. This file is the record the gate did not force.

No code changed in this revision, and no code needs to: the condition has been
in force since the vectors were committed. Only the record is new.

What exactly was taken
----------------------

  - `src/wycheproof_kat_vectors.zig` — 112 860 B, 241 vectors, from
    `testvectors_v1/ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256_p1363_test.json` (241 of its 262
    cases; see the statement of changes for the 21 that were dropped).
  - `src/wycheproof_der_kat_vectors.zig` — 245 455 B, 484 vectors, from
    `testvectors_v1/ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256_test.json` (all 484 cases).

    Total: 725 vectors, 358 315 B. Both fetched from
    https://raw.githubusercontent.com/C2SP/wycheproof/main/ ; the JSON files
    themselves record their provenance as `google-wycheproof` version `0.9rc5`
    for 702 of the 725 rows, plus 16 rows from
    `github/davidben/ecdsa-r-s-edge-cases` 0.1, 4 from
    `github/davidben/ecdsa-s-pow2` 0.1 and 3 from
    `github/davidben/ecdsa-non-minimal-tag` 0.1, all redistributed by
    C2SP/wycheproof under its own licence.

It is not only numbers. The vectors carry 330 distinct authored English
description strings — "edge case for Shamir multiplication", "appending unused
0's to r", "signature malleability", "Signature encoding contains incorrect
types: r=-1, s=Null" — reproduced verbatim as each vector's `.comment` field:

    grep -ho '\.comment = "[^"]*"' modules/p256/src/wycheproof_*_kat_vectors.zig \
      | LC_ALL=C sort -u | wc -l          ->  330

That is authorial prose, not a fact with one possible expression, so the
merger-doctrine reasoning the root NOTICE applies to specification-dictated hex
constants does not reach it. (Use `LC_ALL=C`: under a locale whose collation
ignores punctuation, `sort -u` folds distinct rows together and undercounts
these to 303.)

Verified, not assumed. On 2026-09-06 both upstream JSON files were re-fetched
and compared field by field against the committed Zig, matched on upstream
`tcId`:

    field           identical
    pubkey_hex      725 / 725
    msg_hex         725 / 725
    sig_hex         725 / 725
    comment         725 / 725   (718 byte-identical; 7 differ only by the
                                 documented `"` -> `'` substitution)
    should_verify   725 / 725   (against upstream `result`)

No hex payload and no `tcId` differs from upstream anywhere in either file.

